**Excellent!** This sandbox reproduces the bug LOCALLY: `flutter test` fails with "Building native assets failed" — exactly the CI failure. And I now know the cache layout:

- Shared output dir: `.dart_tool/hooks_runner/shared/sqlite3/build/` (note: NOT under the hashed invocation dir — it's under `hooks_runner/shared/<package>/build/`)
- The download cache subdir: `download-<hash8>` where hash8 = first 8 chars of the expected sha256 (e.g. `download-0f947ebe` for `libsqlite3.x64.linux.so` whose hash starts `0f947ebe...`).

Let me look at the local failure and see the state of the shared dir — it may contain the poisoned/error page file already cached locally from a previous attempt (interesting: `download-0f947ebe` exists already!). Wait — `download-0f947ebe` — the hook downloads into `download-<hash8>/<fileName>`; if a bad (error-page) file got cached there, EVERY subsequent run re-checks it: hash mismatch → re-download... Actually look at the code again:

```dart
final downloadedFile = File(p.join(dir.path, fileName));
if (downloadedFile.existsSync()) {
 // check hash; if match, reuse; else fall through to fetch again
}
final tmp = File('${downloadedFile.path}.tmp');
await fetch(...).pipe(tmp.openWrite());
tmp.renameSync(downloadedFile.path);
``

So a bad cached file just re-triggers a download each time (no poison issue). Good.

